id: k8s.logs
kind: kubernetes
title: Pod logs
aliases: kubectl logs, container logs
tags: operations, diagnostics, observability
questions:
  - how do you read logs from a crashing pod?
  - a service returns 500s — how do you read the failing pods' logs?
invariant: Container stdout/stderr is captured per container and is lost when the container is deleted unless shipped elsewhere.
answer_30s: `kubectl logs` reads the captured stdout/stderr; `--previous` reads the crashed container instance, and `-f` follows a live one.
answer_2m: |
  Understand what you are reading: one stream per container, per Pod, stored on
  the node. `-c <container>` for multi-container Pods, `--previous` for the
  instance that died, `--tail` and `--since` to bound volume. A Pod stuck in
  CrashLoopBackOff may have no current logs at all -- previous is the one that
  matters. In production this is the fallback path; the real path is a
  collection pipeline (Fluent Bit / Vector -> Loki or a managed service), and
  the interview-worthy point is that logs must outlive the Pod, since the Pod
  that explains the incident is usually already gone.
when_to_use:
  - first look at any application-level failure
commands:
  - kubectl logs api-7d9f-abc => what the current container printed
  - kubectl logs api-7d9f-abc --previous => what the container printed before it crashed
  - kubectl logs -f deploy/api => follow all replicas of a Deployment while you poke it
  - kubectl logs -l app=api --tail=200 => bounded multi-Pod read during an incident
failure_modes:
  - Pod deleted before logs were shipped, so the evidence is gone
  - secrets written into logs by request logging
related:
  - k8s.exec
  - design.observability
  - k8s.debugging-workflow
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_logs/
